::ENG3_U06_L07_Q01::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A multimedia product includes writing, a source chart, and a short spoken introduction.
Which choice best shows integration?{
~Each part uses a different topic.#Incorrect. Different topics make the product confusing.
=Each part supports the same research claim for the same audience.#Correct. Integration means the parts work together toward one purpose.
~The chart is decorative and not explained.#Incorrect. Unexplained decoration does not support communication.
~The introduction avoids mentioning the written claim.#Incorrect. The spoken part should connect to the product's purpose.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q02::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student revises a published summary for classmates.
What should the student prioritize?{
~Making the page as bright as possible#Incorrect. Bright design does not guarantee audience understanding.
~Adding unrelated media to fill empty space#Incorrect. Unrelated media weakens publication quality.
~Using vague words so the tone feels casual#Incorrect. Vague words reduce clarity.
=Clarifying the claim, evidence, explanation, and media captions#Correct. These revisions make the product clearer and more useful for readers.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A product uses research from a secondary source.
What should be included before publication?{
=A source note or citation that shows where the research came from#Correct. Source information supports credibility and responsible publication.
~Only a decorative border around the source information#Incorrect. Decoration does not identify the source.
~No source information because the product is digital#Incorrect. Digital publication still requires responsible source use.
~A hidden note that readers cannot see#Incorrect. Source information must be accessible enough to support credibility.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q04::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student selects a video clip for a published product.
Which reason is strongest?{
~The clip is popular online.#Incorrect. Popularity is not the same as audience support.
~The clip is long enough to fill time.#Incorrect. Length is not a quality reason.
=The clip clarifies a research point that the written summary explains.#Correct. The clip is useful because it supports the writing and audience understanding.
~The clip is unrelated but entertaining.#Incorrect. Entertainment without relevance distracts from publication purpose.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q05::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A final product has many convention errors in captions.
Why is this a problem?{
~Captions should never be used.#Incorrect. Captions are useful when they explain multimedia.
=Errors can distract the audience and reduce credibility.#Correct. Publication-ready communication requires careful conventions in all visible text.
~Errors only matter in printed essays.#Incorrect. Digital and multimedia products also require standard conventions.
~Errors make captions more creative.#Incorrect. Errors usually weaken clarity and trust.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q06::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student identifies the audience as "classmates who have not read my research notes."
What should the product include?{
=Brief context and clear explanation before detailed evidence#Correct. This audience needs enough background to understand the claim and evidence.
~Only advanced terms without explanation#Incorrect. Unexplained terms may confuse classmates.
~A list of sources with no summary#Incorrect. Readers need explanation, not only source titles.
~Only images with no writing#Incorrect. The writing is needed to communicate the research claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q07::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A product has a strong written claim but unrelated images.
What is the best revision?{
~Keep the images because all multimedia is helpful.#Incorrect. Multimedia must support the audience's understanding.
~Remove the claim so the images become the focus.#Incorrect. The claim is central to the product.
~Add more unrelated images.#Incorrect. More unrelated media increases distraction.
=Replace the images with media that supports the claim or explain a relevant connection.#Correct. The multimedia must connect clearly to the writing and purpose.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q08::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student prepares to export a LAN publication.
Which final check is most useful?{
~Did I use the fanciest font available?#Incorrect. Fancy fonts can reduce readability.
=Can the intended audience read, understand, and navigate the product?#Correct. Audience access and understanding are essential before export.
~Did I include every note I took?#Incorrect. Too much information can overwhelm readers.
~Can I skip proofreading because it is digital?#Incorrect. Digital publication still needs proofreading.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q09::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student includes oral speaker notes with a digital product.
How should the notes support publication readiness?{
~They should introduce a new topic not found in the product.#Incorrect. New unrelated content creates confusion.
~They should replace all written explanation.#Incorrect. Notes may support communication, but publication-ready writing still matters.
=They should reinforce the same claim and guide audience understanding.#Correct. Speaker notes are useful when they support the same purpose as the product.
~They should avoid mentioning the audience.#Incorrect. Audience awareness is central to communication.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q10::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A product uses academic vocabulary.
Which use is strongest?{
=The vocabulary is precise and explained when the audience may need support.#Correct. Academic vocabulary should improve clarity and be understandable to the audience.
~The vocabulary is difficult only to sound impressive.#Incorrect. Unnecessary difficulty can confuse the audience.
~The vocabulary is unrelated to the claim.#Incorrect. Vocabulary should support the topic and purpose.
~The vocabulary replaces evidence.#Incorrect. Precise terms do not replace support and explanation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q11::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student says, "My final product has writing and media, so it is ready."
What is missing from that reasoning?{
~A promise to make the product longer#Incorrect. Length is not the missing issue.
~A requirement that every product use video#Incorrect. Video is not always necessary.
=A check that writing and media are revised, connected, and audience appropriate#Correct. Publication readiness depends on quality and integration, not just having parts.
~A rule that removes all source notes#Incorrect. Source notes support credibility.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q12::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student wants to use a chart from research.
Which action best supports audience understanding?{
~Paste the chart without explanation.#Incorrect. Readers need a connection to the claim.
~Shrink the chart so it fits in a corner.#Incorrect. If the chart is unreadable, it cannot help the audience.
=Label the chart and explain how it supports the written claim.#Correct. Labels and explanation integrate the chart into the publication.
~Use the chart only because charts look academic.#Incorrect. Appearance is not enough.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q13::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A published page has a strong source note but unclear writing.
What should the student revise?{
=The writing, so the audience can understand the claim and evidence#Correct. Source notes matter, but the writing must still be clear.
~Only the source note, because writing clarity does not matter#Incorrect. Writing quality is central to publication.
~Only the color scheme#Incorrect. Color does not fix unclear content.
~Nothing, because one strong part makes the product ready#Incorrect. Publication readiness requires all key parts to work.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q14::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is publishing for a school audience.
Which tone is most appropriate?{
~Careless and unclear#Incorrect. Publication should be understandable and purposeful.
=Clear, respectful, and academically appropriate#Correct. This tone fits a school publication and supports credibility.
~Dismissive of readers#Incorrect. Dismissive tone weakens audience connection.
~Full of unexplained private jokes#Incorrect. Private jokes can confuse readers outside the writer's group.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q15::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student adds a caption: "This picture is here because it looked good."
What revision is best?{
~This picture is here because pictures are required.#Incorrect. This still does not explain purpose.
~This picture is here because the slide was empty.#Incorrect. Filling space is not a strong publication reason.
~This picture is here and needs no explanation.#Incorrect. Readers need a meaningful connection.
=This image helps readers visualize the setting that shapes the character's decision.#Correct. This caption connects the media to audience understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q16::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is choosing between two final products.
Which one is more publication ready?{
=The product with clear writing, purposeful media, source notes, and audience-aware design#Correct. These qualities show readiness for publication.
~The product with the most images but little explanation#Incorrect. Many images do not replace clarity.
~The product with no revision after feedback#Incorrect. Revision is part of quality preparation.
~The product with hidden source information#Incorrect. Source information should support reader trust.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q17::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: Feedback says a published summary assumes readers know the text.
What should the student add?{
~More decorative design#Incorrect. Design does not solve the context problem.
~A new unrelated argument#Incorrect. A new argument may confuse the focus.
=Brief context that helps readers understand the claim and evidence#Correct. Audience-aware publication gives readers the background they need.
~A shorter file name only#Incorrect. File names do not explain the text.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q18::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A multimedia product includes three different claims.
What is the likely issue?{
~It is automatically stronger because it has more claims.#Incorrect. Too many claims may weaken focus.
~It should include more media before revision.#Incorrect. Media will not solve a focus problem.
~It needs no audience check.#Incorrect. Audience fit is always important.
=It may lack focus and confuse the audience about the main purpose.#Correct. Publication-ready communication needs a clear central purpose.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q19::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student plans to publish without previewing the final page.
Why is previewing important?{
~It lets the student add random graphics.#Incorrect. Previewing is not for adding unrelated decoration.
=It helps catch readability, formatting, source, and media problems before submission.#Correct. Previewing supports final publication quality.
~It replaces revision completely.#Incorrect. Previewing is part of the revision and checking process.
~It makes the audience unnecessary.#Incorrect. Previewing helps the writer think about the audience.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q20::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A product uses a quotation from research.
What should the student do?{
~Leave the quotation unexplained.#Incorrect. Readers need to understand how the quotation supports the claim.
~Use the quotation only as decoration.#Incorrect. Evidence should support the argument or explanation.
=Explain the quotation and connect it to the product's purpose.#Correct. Evidence must be integrated into the communication product.
~Remove all source information.#Incorrect. Source information supports credibility.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q21::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is deciding whether a product is ready to publish.
Which question is best?{
=Will my intended audience understand the claim, evidence, media, and purpose?#Correct. This question checks the main parts of publication-ready communication.
~Is the product longer than required?#Incorrect. Length does not prove readiness.
~Did I use every possible media type?#Incorrect. Not every media type is necessary.
~Can I avoid revising the writing?#Incorrect. Revision is part of publication readiness.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q22::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A final product has an excellent image but no written claim.
What is the best correction?{
~Let the image serve as the full argument.#Incorrect. An image alone usually does not provide a complete research claim.
~Add more images instead of writing.#Incorrect. More images do not create a clear claim.
~Publish because images are enough.#Incorrect. Publication-ready communication needs clear writing.
=Add a clear written claim and connect the image to that claim.#Correct. The writing and media should work together.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q23::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student revises after TOR notes that the product is hard to navigate.
Which revision helps most?{
~Make the text smaller.#Incorrect. Smaller text can make navigation harder.
=Use clear headings, logical order, and readable formatting.#Correct. These changes help the audience move through the product.
~Remove all captions.#Incorrect. Captions help explain media.
~Add unrelated links.#Incorrect. Unrelated links create distraction.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q24::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student needs one final sentence explaining readiness.
Which sentence is strongest?{
~My product is ready because I am tired of revising.#Incorrect. Readiness is based on quality, not fatigue.
~My product is ready because it has a lot of parts.#Incorrect. Many parts do not prove integration.
=My product is ready because the revised writing, source support, and multimedia all help my audience understand the claim.#Correct. This sentence explains readiness using audience, writing, research, and multimedia.
~My product is ready because the pictures are large.#Incorrect. Picture size alone does not show publication quality.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_Q25::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student completes the last check before export.
Which action best matches the lesson standard?{
=Confirm that the product is tailored to the audience, integrates multimedia, and is ready for online or LAN publication.#Correct. This directly matches the focus of publication-ready multimedia communication.
~Confirm that the product has no writing.#Incorrect. Quality writing is required.
~Confirm that all media is decorative.#Incorrect. Multimedia should support understanding.
~Confirm that audience needs were ignored.#Incorrect. Audience tailoring is central to the standard.
}
